    {
      "dom_id": "question-80354",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252935/assets/7bcc2f587bff8042ea087d8a3ba3ea8be5d7de513cdc3bd37f2a1cafdb4e278a.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-x(x+1)(x+3)^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=-1,0$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=-1,0$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-3$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$",
