      "dom_id": "question-80361",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252935/assets/0bf56fdb429d050465875f1c885aa83a36fb4fd7bfe4709fcda11db1a3574a2d.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-{x}^{4}(x+1)(x-2)^{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0$ is $4,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=-1$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $3,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a triple-root, the graph must be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{III}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{III}.$",
